must* have a '3' in the parent row, at the midpoint, in order for the \"child\" '6' or '7' to form. I'm testing this hypothesis to make sure I have the exact rule.\n\n\n**Verifying the Midpoint**\n\nI've rigorously validated the midpoint condition. My focus is now sharp, I've confirmed that the critical pattern is indeed \\[C, 3, C], where C can be any character (i.e., a '7') and the midpoint value *must* be '3' to trigger the \"child\" generation, either '6' becomes a '7' or a '7' becomes a '6'. I've exhaustively checked Train 1 and Train 3's corresponding pairs.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Relationships**\n\nI'm now focusing on the pattern's evolution. Observing the example, I've confirmed that the critical pattern is indeed \\[C, 3, C], with C being either 7 or 6. If such a pattern exists in a row, a child is generated in the next row at the midpoint, either the '7' becomes '6', or the '6' becomes '7'. I'm currently verifying this thoroughly across different rows.\n\n\n",
